      "evidenceText": "The Kicker - Columbia Journalism Review a b A CJR Podcast The Kicker The Kicker is a podcast on the media and the world today. It comes out twice a month, hosted by Megan Greenwell for the Columbia Journalism Review . It is available wherever you get your podcasts, including Apple Podcasts and Spotify , as well as on YouTube .   Is It Crazy to Go to Journalism School? November 12, 2026 By Megan Greenwell A student reflects on leaving the law to pursue his writing dreams. Magazines Are Back, and They’re Newsletters October 29, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Preeya Goenka on Beehiiv’s growing media portfolio. California’s Big Bet on Government-Funded News October 15, 2026 By Megan Greenwell How Matt Pearce, a journalist turned policymaker, helped create a landmark new law. A Victim of the ‘Elon Musk Playbook’ October 1, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Alison Gerber, a longtime editor at the Chattanooga Times Free Press , on losing her job after twenty-three years. Journalism Philanthropy, but Make It Fun September 17, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Ruth Ann Harnisch on life as the fairy godmother of newsletters, podcasts, and blogs. So Are Nonfiction Books Dead or Not? September 3, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Anna Sproul-Latimer, a literary agent, on what a shrinking market means for journalists. The Journalist Who Became a Doula August 20, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Kenya Hunter took a terrifying leap out of journalism. She couldn’t be happier. Twenty Years of Food Blogs August 6, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Smitten Kitchen ’s Deb Perelman has never needed AI to check a recipe. What Went Wrong July 23, 2026 By Megan Greenwell A first-time editor in chief reflects on a startup that didn’t work. Welcome to Hell July 9, 2026 By Megan Greenwell And now for something completely different—a site with personality big enough for the city it covers. Why Do You Have to Run from Us? June 30, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Local reporters are struggling to get answers from the politicians they cover. No Fanboys Need Apply June 23, 2026 By Megan Greenwell In a sea of tech coverage that reads like advertorials, Tim Marchman and Wired bare real teeth. When a Bigger League Means Less Access June 11, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Sports Illustrated ’s Emma Baccellieri on covering the changing world of women’s basketball. ‘We Are on the Platforms Immigrants Are On’ May 21, 2026 By Megan Greenwell How Documented is reinventing immigration coverage. The Town May 7, 2026 By Megan Greenwell The Globe ’s Emily Sweeney breaks out of Boston. Taking Back Saturday April 23, 2026 By Megan Greenwell “We’re sports people. We like to score.” Student, Teacher April 9, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Eric Gustafson on fighting for journalistic integrity at every level. The Inside Look March 26, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Chatting with the New York Times’ trust editor. Lessons from an Early-Career Journalist March 12, 2026 By Megan Greenwell “I wouldn’t be getting this experience at a national publication, to be extensively following the hottest news story in the country.” A Look Back at Covering Gaza for the Post February 26, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Since October 7, 2023, Miriam Berger has been on assignment in Jerusalem, covering Israel, Palestine, and war. A few weeks ago, she learned she and hundreds of colleagues were being laid off. The Letter of the Law, and the Law in Practice February 12, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Experts discuss the risks posed to journalism as the courts test the limits of press freedom law. A Veteran of the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette —and Its Long Strike—Prepares for What’s Next January 29, 2026 By Megan Greenwell “We didn’t go on strike to put the paper out of business. We went on strike to keep it good.” Jasper Wang’s Unvarnished Truth January 15, 2026 By Megan Greenwell Worker ownership is exciting. It’s also not, he says, a “panacea.” Why You Should Never Marry a Journalist—and Other Lessons from Decades in Media January 8, 2026 By Megan Greenwell The Kicker returns with our former host, Josh Hersh, and our new one, Megan Greenwell, in conversation. Margaret Sullivan Takes a New Look at Journalism Ethics October 23, 2025 By Josh Hersh The Columbia professor talks about her new series about how the challenges facing journalists have evolved with the times. Chicago’s Block Club Is Ready for ICE October 10, 2025 By Josh Hersh The independent digital startup has been on the ground covering ICE’s surge and the attacks on journalists. Elle Reeve on the Charlie Kirk Shooting Suspect’s Inscrutable Memes September 19, 2025 By Josh Hersh The CNN correspondent, who specializes in fringe and internet subcultures, on how to make sense of the killing. Garrett Graff Thinks the Press Should Be Taking Trump’s Health Much More Seriously September 10, 2025 By Josh Hersh The author and historian on political coverage in unprecedented times. Hind Hassan Is Sorry We Didn’t Do More to Make Journalism Safe August 28, 2025 By Josh Hersh “We’re responsible for what our industry stands for,” the documentary news reporter says. Will the Jeffrey Epstein Scandal Break MAGA Media? August 1, 2025 By Josh Hersh Mother Jones ’s Anna Merlan on how the Epstein files are tearing MAGA media apart. What’s the Matter with the BBC? July 11, 2025 By Josh Hersh Former Guardian editor Alan Rusbridger on the challenges facing the venerable British institution. Live from Tbilisi June 23, 2025 By Josh Hersh Conversations from the Zeg Storytelling Festival. What’s the Point of Investigating Trump? June 17, 2025 By Josh Hersh The Times ’ David Fahrenthold on the value of investigative reporting in a time of shamelessness. ‘I Try to Find the Question That People Cannot Squirm Out Of’ May 29, 2025 By Josh Hersh Nashville’s Phil Williams on local investigative reporting. ‘The Threat Is Very Real’ May 9, 2025 By Josh Hersh NPR’s Katherine Maher on the fight to save public media. Inside El Salvador’s Dystopian Prison Network April 24, 2025 By Josh Hersh A reporter who covered the gang crackdown on why budding authoritarians like what they see. Kai Ryssdal Was America’s Economic Voice of Reason This Week April 11, 2025 By Josh Hersh The Marketplace host on Trump’s economic policies, and telling it like it is. Carlos Watson Goes Free: A Surprising Coda to the CJR Podcast April 4, 2025 By Josh Hersh Prosecutors weren’t notified in advance, witnesses are in shock—and Watson’s family celebrates his freedom. Molly White Knows You Don’t Understand Crypto March 28, 2025 By Josh Hersh The researcher and newsletter writer on one of DC’s must-know topics. The Legal War on Journalism March 12, 2025 By Josh Hersh How Trump’s attacks on the press are already affecting publishers. A Spring Training Palate Cleanser February 27, 2025 By Josh Hersh An update on the state of sports journalism, with the Baltimore Banner ’s Chris Korman. A Warning from a Hungarian Journalist: ‘Brace Yourself for the Worst’ February 14, 2025 By Josh Hersh András Pethő has watched American media head down a familiar path. Back in the Whirlwind January 31, 2025 By Josh Hersh CJR’s Jon Allsop on how the media handled Trump’s first week back—and what’s still to come. A Wild Start to the Year January 14, 2025 By Josh Hersh Coda’s Natalia Antelava on Meta, Trump, and how journalism can survive 2025. The Unraveling of Ozy Media December 9, 2024 By Susie Banikarim , Josh Hersh The trial of Carlos Watson and the excesses of the digital media age. How Trump Won Over the Latino Vote November 8, 2024 By Josh Hersh A deep dive with CJR contributor Jack Herrera. The end of endorsements at the Post October 30, 2024 By Josh Hersh Former editor Martin Baron on Jeff Bezos and editorial independence What Trump might do if he loses October 25, 2024 By Josh Hersh NBC’s Ryan Reilly on the future of “stop the steal” A manic news cycle in New York October 9, 2024 By Josh Hersh Hell Gate ’s Chris Robbins on the new New York media landscape The power of uncomfortable ideas September 18, 2024 By Josh Hersh Jina Moore Ngarambe on her time at Guernica ‘That’s how you run a debate!’ September 4, 2024 By Josh Hersh 9News’s Kyle Clark on holding politicians accountable A long, destructive path August 26, 2024 By Josh Hersh Source NM ’s Patrick Lohmann on the never-ending wildfire season ’68 state August 15, 2024 By Josh Hersh Heather Hendershot and Ted Koppel on a year for the media to remember Kamala steps up July 25, 2024 By Josh Hersh Politico ’s Eugene Daniels on a wild month of news Spanish-speaking journalism—in Iowa July 15, 2024 By Josh Hersh A conversation with Lorena López What did we know and when? July 3, 2024 By Josh Hersh Alex Thompson on Biden’s mental decline Not even ‘no comment’ June 24, 2024 By Josh Hersh Paul Farhi on the media’s struggles with relevance Staying scrupulously neutral June 18, 2024 By Josh Hersh Steve Herman on covering the White House in the age of Trump Haaretz ’s unique role May 17, 2024 By Josh Hersh How Israeli journalists are covering the country Crisis at Columbia May 4, 2024 By Josh Hersh A conversation with Jelani Cobb Talking to the minds behind ‘Inside Wagner’ April 25, 2024 By Josh Hersh Video journalism unmasks Russia’s secretive mercenary group ‘Self-regulated cartels’ April 3, 2024 By Josh Hersh How to revive investigative sports reporting in the age of the athlete All Kicker episodes » The voice of journalism, since 1961 About Mission Masthead Privacy Policy Contact Support CJR Become a Member Donate Advertise Contact Us Copyright 2026, Columbia Journalism Review",
