      "finalUrl": "https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2026-11-04/democrats-historic-rout-was-fueled-by-massive-voter-swing-to-left",
      "contentType": "text/html;charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Democrats' historic rout was fueled by massive voter swing to the left - Los Angeles Times",
      "pageDescription": "Not since voters were repulsed by President Nixon after the Watergate scandal half a century ago has the popular vote swung so decisively in a single election cycle.",
      "evidenceText": "Democrats' historic rout was fueled by massive voter swing to the left - Los Angeles Times Advertisement Politics Democrats’ historic rout was fueled by massive voter swing to the left Abdul El-Sayed is joined by his wife, Sarah Jukaku, at an election night event after winning the Michigan Senate race. (Julia Demaree Nikhinson / Associated Press) By  Justine McDaniel  and  Michael Wilner Nov. 4, 2026 Updated  1:27 PM PT 132 8 min Click here to listen to this article Share via Close extra sharing options Email Facebook X LinkedIn Threads Reddit WhatsApp Copy Link URL Copied! Print 0:00 0:00 1x This is read by an automated voice. Please report any issues or inconsistencies here . See more from the L.A. Times in Google Search. Set us as preferred Margins outstripped expectations, posting double-digit shifts to the left by nearly every voting bloc. Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis said it was ‘close to an extinction-level event for Republicans across the country.’ p]:text-cms-story-body-color-text\" data-dateline data-subscriber-content> WASHINGTON —  Democrats heralded a new era in American politics Wednesday after voters broke Republicans’ control of government and delivered a pivotal rebuke of President Trump. Victories that handed Democrats control of the House and Senate on Tuesday night came in historic form, with margins outstripping expectations and double-digit shifts to the left by nearly every voting bloc. In some of the reddest states in the union, even Republican redistricting efforts designed to shield districts from competition were not enough to prevent Democratic inroads. The sweeping wins were driven by a rare coalition of voters across nearly all walks of American life, in cities and countryside, from the rural Midwest and Deep South to a traditionally conservative mid-Atlantic. Not since voters were repulsed by President Nixon after the Watergate scandal half a century ago has the popular vote swung so decisively in a single election cycle. Advertisement The wave was so comprehensive that it handed Democrats wins that for decades have been unthinkable — flipping Senate seats in Kansas, South Carolina and Texas — and a majority of governors’ seats for the first time since 2010. The party also flipped statehouse chambers in nine states, including Texas, Arizona and Georgia. The size of the House majority remained to be seen as states continued counting votes, but Democrats had reached 237 seats, representing a decisive wave, by Wednesday afternoon, according to Associated Press projections. World & Nation Speaker Johnson won’t seek reelection as House Republican leader after party’s midterm losses House Republicans are expected to elect their new leader when members-elect gather in Washington next week, in what probably will be a familiar showdown between two who reached for the top spot before — Reps. Steve Scalise of Louisiana and Jim Jordan of Ohio. Nov. 4, 2026 “It wasn’t simply a blue wave, it was an American tsunami,” House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries said, pledging to deliver “a dramatic change in direction” after the new legislative session begins in January. “The American people have chosen the economy over extremism. The American people have chosen hope over hatred.” Advertisement The routing of the GOP appeared to validate the populist messages Democrats — in many cases, both progressives and moderates — ran on, from lowering costs to addressing healthcare and housing issues to confronting the power of corporations in American society. And Democrats moved quickly to acknowledge that their big wins come with demands from voters — and the next two years will be a test of how they deliver on them. “Last night we saw how quickly a political party can go from a big victory to crushing defeat if they sell out and fail to deliver,” Rep. Greg Casar of Texas, chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus, told reporters on a call. “So now it’s up to Democrats to show that we will actually deliver what is being asked of us.” As the electoral map came into clearer focus, members of both parties predicted the results would reshape politics for years. Speaking to reporters Wednesday, Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer (D-N.Y.) called it “a generational remake of the Senate map that is going to change America not only now but on into the future.” He pledged Democratic senators would be intent on working for their constituents and delivering on promises to relieve Americans’ economic “anguish” and clean up government corruption. He also nodded to the responsibility the party now shoulders. Advertisement “With this majority, Americans have given we Democrats the opportunity to prove ourselves worthy of their trust, and that’s our most important job,” Schumer said. Democrats had won 54 Senate seats with two left to be called, Alaska and Mississippi. The race in Mississippi, a seat that was considered likely safe for Republicans, was too close to call, a sign of how deep the Democrats’ reach had gone. In every demographic, whether cut by gender, race, age, education or region, voters shifted to the left compared to 2024, in most cases by double digits, according to New York Times data . Men 18 to 44 moved to the left by 31 percentage points, Latinos by 28 points, voters under 30 by 32 points. The nation also elected several historic firsts, including the first Muslim senator, the first openly gay male senator and the first Black female governor, in spite of Republican campaign attacks around identity. And Democrats defied Republican redistricting efforts in at least 11 districts, including four in Florida , four in Texas , one each in Ohio , Alabama and North Carolina . Democrats had been cautiously optimistic heading into the evening, with good polling and the American tradition of midterm backlash against a sitting president behind them. But few had expected gains of this magnitude. “The only thing unusual here is scale,” said Andrew Sinclair, an assistant professor of government at Claremont McKenna College. “It is not at all unusual in American political history to see a poorly led party lose badly in the next election.” Advertisement “To build anything enduring out of this election,” he added, “Democrats are going to have to do the hard work of having honest conversations with each other about how such a diverse group of people can cooperate and deliver results.” The results showed the success of economic populist messages, said Alex Jacquez, a Democratic consultant and former policy advisor to Sen. Bernie Sanders (I-Vt.). “The throughline for all the Democrats [who won] is the corporate-friendliness era of moderation is over and we are much more squarely in the prairie populism of the ’80s and ’90s,” Jacquez said. “You saw a real resurgence of that.” The unambiguous nature of the victory helped repel the notion that Republicans might attempt to challenge election results , as Trump had telegraphed as a possibility before the election. Instead, the president spoke at length about construction on his ballroom on Wednesday. Trump blamed the fact that he was not on the ballot for the losses, as Republicans swiftly began trading blame — at times directing it toward Trump and the war in Iran. Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis, a likely 2028 presidential contender, predicted the results could “reverberate for the next decade or more,” saying the routing was worse than anticipated. Advertisement It was “close to an extinction-level event for Republicans across the country,” DeSantis told reporters. Republicans will now face questions about the party’s future direction, and how they prevent a repeat of Tuesday’s wave in 2028. President Trump signaled his willingness to accept the election results. California ‘Not a hit on me’: Trump faults candidates and Fox News, not himself, for Republican losses Reflecting on the losses, Trump largely put the onus on the candidates, some of whom he said he only endorsed for the good of the party. Nov. 4, 2026 “For Republicans, it is clear that President Trump has led the party to disaster — again,” Sinclair said. “Looking to the future, Republicans should reconsider following his style of governance and his policy agenda.” Democrats will now have to deliver on the affordability and accountability agenda. With full control of Congress, they will be able to send legislation to Trump’s desk, and could force him to sign bills or make unpopular vetoes. Schumer told reporters Wednesday that the party would work to lower costs “with the president or without him, period.” “It is critically important now that Democrats, now that we’ve been given the keys to the car here, we’re able to drive it,” Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro, who won reelection, said on MS NOW’s “Morning Joe.” Shapiro, also a likely presidential contender, projected confidence in the new majorities and said Democrats would need to show that they “can legislate, bring down costs, end these wars.” Advertisement On the call with progressive House Democrats, Casar suggested that the party should send a bill to Trump’s desk every week on something “overwhelmingly popular” with Americans and should surmount administrative hurdles that can keep Congress from moving quickly. The mood among Democrats was quickly turning to “resolute” amid the jubilation, said consultant Andrew DeStefano. Party operatives want to build lasting power, he said, not become just another swing in the electoral pendulum. “Folks don’t want this to be the latest ping-pong volley,” DeStefano said. Democrats are “laser-focused on how we can deliver on some achievable concrete policy.” More to Read Democrats flock to Denver for a post-midterms introspection-turned-celebration Nov. 13, 2026 Democrats flip Georgia state House, giving them control of at least 12 legislative chambers Nov. 9, 2026 Democrats win the Arizona House, Senate and governor’s office for the first time since 1966 Nov. 6, 2026 Show Comments Politics World & Nation Midterm Election 2026 Deeply reported insights into legislation, politics and policy from Sacramento, Washington and beyond. In your inbox twice per week. By continuing, you agree to our Terms of Service , which include arbitration and a class action waiver. You agree that we and our third-party vendors may collect and use your information, including through cookies, pixels and similar technologies, for the purposes set forth in our Privacy Policy such as personalizing your experience and ads. Login or register with email Agree & Continue Justine McDaniel Follow Us X Email Bluesky Justine McDaniel is a Washington correspondent for the Los Angeles Times, covering politics, policy and the Trump administration’s ongoing battles with California. She previously covered national news and politics at the Washington Post. Her previous reporting on transportation, water contamination and other topics at the Philadelphia Inquirer was recognized with several awards. She was a 2014 reporting fellow for News21, part of the Carnegie-Knight Initiative on the Future of Journalism Education. She grew up in the North Bay. Michael Wilner Follow Us X Email Michael Wilner is the Washington bureau chief for the Los Angeles Times. He has spent more than a decade covering the federal government with a focus on the White House. Before joining The Times, Wilner was chief Washington correspondent for McClatchy, where his enterprise and international reporting was recognized with multiple awards. He previously covered foreign affairs in Washington and overseas for the Jerusalem Post. Wilner is a graduate of Claremont McKenna College and Columbia University and is a native of New York City. More From the Los Angeles Times World & Nation Trump’s handpicked GOP chair faces rebellion after midterm landslide Nov. 15, 2026 Politics Facing corruption investigations, Trump calls his critics ‘corrupt’ Nov. 15, 2026 Voices Commentary: Newsom, Hegseth, Becerra — who’s in the hottest water? Our columnists